--TEST--
`Core\Net\Stream::read` answers no more than `$max` and leaves the remainder on the socket, so a short answer is the ordinary case rather than a failure — `rule:core-classes/net-one-api-three-transports`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `$max` is a ceiling on one read, not a length to fill: a socket reports what
// arrived. This case asserts both sides of that at once — the first read stops
// at `$max` with octets still waiting, and the loop after it collects exactly
// the rest, so a member that ignored its ceiling and a member that threw the
// remainder away both fail here while looking right on either line alone.
var $door = Core\Net::listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
var $client = Core\Net::connect("127.0.0.1", $door->port(), 5s);
var $served = $door->accept(5s);

$client->write("abcdefgh" as bytes, 5s);

// Eight octets went out in one write, so eight are waiting; what makes this
// line the ceiling claim is that three come back and not eight.
tainted bytes $first = $served->read(3, 5s);
echo "first: ", $first as string, "\n";

// And the remainder is still there — counted rather than compared, because
// nothing here may assume the operating system hands the last five over in one
// answer. That is exactly what a member returning what arrived is not allowed
// to promise, so a case that read one answer and compared it would be pinning
// the platform rather than the member.
uint $rest = 0;
while ($rest < 5) {
    $rest = $rest + Core\Bytes::length($served->read(16, 5s));
}
echo "rest: ", $rest as string, "\n";

// `write` is bounded the same way and answers the count, so a caller with more
// to send than the socket will take loops on that number rather than being
// held inside one call for as long as the peer chooses.
echo "wrote: ", $served->write("ok" as bytes, 5s) as string, "\n";

$served->close();
$client->close();
$door->close();
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The round-trip case's grant, unchanged: what this case varies is the member's
# arguments, not what the deployment allows.
[capabilities.net]
listen = true
connect = ["127.0.0.1"]
internal = ["127.0.0.1"]
--EXPECT--
first: abc
rest: 5
wrote: 2
